{"name":"Agave","hex":"#879d99","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Agave","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Asparagaceae – agaves, of the Americas, principally in arid environments."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Agave"}],"entries":[{"title":"Agave","blocks":[{"text":"Agave is a genus of monocots native to the arid regions of the Americas. The genus is primarily known for its succulent and xerophytic species that typically form large rosettes of strong, fleshy leaves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Agave_americana_R01.jpg/330px-Agave_americana_R01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agave"},{"title":"Agave (daughter of Cadmus)","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Agave, was the daughter of Cadmus and a princess of Thebes. She is most well known for her role in the myths surrounding her nephew, Dionysus, god of wine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Death_Pentheus_Louvre_G445.jpg/330px-Death_Pentheus_Louvre_G445.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agave_(daughter_of_Cadmus)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg/250px-Agave_chiapensis_whole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The large flower spike of Agave chiapensis, San Francisco Botanical Garden","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik